<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Susan B. Anthony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a>–<a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Susan Brownell Anthony</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Susan B. Anthony was an American campaigner for women's rights and the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and the leading organiser of the movement for women's suffrage. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-cady-stanton">Elizabeth Cady Stanton</a> she founded the National Woman Suffrage Association in 1869. Arrested for voting in the presidential election of 1872, she used her trial to argue the case for women's citizenship. She did not live to see the Nineteenth Amendment of 1920, which was widely called the Anthony Amendment.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/susan-b-anthony.jpg" alt="Susan B. Anthony" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SB_Anthony_from_WoC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
